WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.420
- [Waiter] Excuse me,
ma'am, you had the lobster?

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.253
- Ah, yes.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.960
- [Waiter] Some champagne?

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.003
- Yes, please.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:17.420
- [Waiter] Peanuts?

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:19.503
- Peanuts?

00:00:21.017 --> 00:00:23.680
(baby crying)

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.380
- Where's my lobster?

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.450
- [Pilot] Ladies and gentleman,

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:27.810
this is your captain speaking.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.050
We just wanna thank you
for arriving at the airport

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.730
two hours before your flight

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.990
and enduring all those long lines.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.150
You'll be pleased to know we've already

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:36.290
charged your credit card

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:38.360
for that awful turkey
sandwich you ordered,

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:42.000
but don't worry about the
peanuts, those are on the house.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:42.847
- I'll take 'em.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.360
- Flying wasn't always
the headache it is today.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.390
Airlines used to look more
like cocktail parties,

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.230
where everyone dressed up.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.370
Three course meals were served

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:56.870
and you walked off the
plane with travel bags,

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.860
filled with toothpaste, deodorant
and other life essentials.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.170
- Why did it change?

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.090
To answer that we'll need to take a trip

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.970
through history, economics, and politics.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:10.860
So fasten your seat belts
and prepare for takeoff.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.527
(upbeat music)

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.560
- Flying didn't become popular

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.930
until what many call the
golden age of aviation.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.270
There's no clear consensus
on when this began.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:32.690
Some historians date it back
to the 1930s or even earlier.

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:35.280
While others think it
started in the late fifties,

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.180
when jets were integrated into the market.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.200
Regardless of its origins,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.200
passengers during this time
were treated like royalty.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:48.820
Meals included dishes such
as smoked salmon and caviar.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:53.050
Drink offerings ranged from
fancy cocktails to French wines.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:56.350
Some planes even had a winding staircases

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:58.080
into separate rooms.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.980
It was like a better
version of first-class

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.010
except pretty much
everyone was first class.

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:07.830
- As far back as 1938, most
domestic flights in America

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.630
were regulated by the government.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.610
They had control over
fares, routes, schedules,

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.540
investigations, certifications,

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.500
market entry of new airlines.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.220
So pretty much everything.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.810
But with federal regulation
came a lot of turbulence.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:25.220
One of the biggest problems
during this time was cost.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.740
The government wanted to
make sure airlines made more

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:28.940
than enough money.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.910
So unless it was a short trip,

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:34.120
you could expect some
pretty outrageous prices.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.580
- How much exactly?

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.820
To give you an idea, a flight
from Los Angeles to New York

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:43.490
in June of 1959 costs about $170.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:45.300
Sounds like a deal, right?

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.800
Not if you adjust for inflation.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:52.680
In 2021 dollars, that
comes out to over $1,500.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.920
No wonder the service was so great.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.100
They were desperately trying
to get people to dish out

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.840
what, back then, was a
good chunk of their income.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.150
- Unfortunately, high cost
wasn't the only problem.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.040
It also took longer to
reach your destination.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.630
These days you can fly
cross country direct

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.600
or at worst a stop or two.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.780
But back then, one cross country trip

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.460
could have as many as five layovers.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.330
That could double your travel time.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:19.540
- And if you missed your flight,

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.100
you probably couldn't catch
another one until the next day

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:23.970
or even the following week.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.300
Fewer flights existed back then,

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.970
and airlines had to apply for new routes,

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:31.760
which in some cases took
years to get approved.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.300
If they got approved at all.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.950
On top of that, you had to
deal with passengers smoking.

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.640
Something that wasn't completely outlawed

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.930
until the year 2000.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:44.140
Planes also tended to be
much louder back then,

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.233
and many weren't able to
fly over extreme weather.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.370
So it wasn't uncommon for turbulence

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:56.350
to cause broken glass,
injuries and air sickness.

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:58.790
- Safety was another major concern.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.120
Today it's incredibly rare

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.080
for a commercial airline to crash.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.520
Back then it happened
half a dozen times a year.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:11.300
And in some years, hijackings
happened on a weekly basis.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.120
- Thankfully in the
1970s, politicians said

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.540
enough is enough.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.610
The recent bankruptcy of the
heavily regulated railroads

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:20.760
convinced Congress of what economists

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.480
had been seeing for decades,

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.740
that it was the regulation itself

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:27.580
causing higher costs and inefficiencies.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.860
- The Carter administration finally agreed

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:33.260
that everyone would benefit
from new players in the market,

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:34.670
less control over routes,

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.460
and the abolishment of price regulations.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.990
But this didn't sit well with the airlines

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:43.070
since it meant their income
wouldn't be guaranteed.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:46.010
- But their plea to maintain
the rigid status quo

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:47.530
was unconvincing.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.780
And in 1978, the government
began its departure

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:52.790
out of the airline industry.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:56.300
Well, aside from safety inspections
and air traffic control,

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.280
which it still handles to this day.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:01.320
- As expected deregulation
led to competition

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:02.153
in the marketplace,

00:05:02.153 --> 00:05:04.360
which naturally led to lower costs

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.820
since everyone was
competing for customers.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:11.410
Plane tickets, including
fees, dropped by 50%

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.420
between 1979 and 2011.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:17.000
Causing the number of
passengers to triple,

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.050
which is great for fliers,
not so much for the airlines.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:24.300
Eight major carriers and
over 100 smaller ones

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.570
went bankrupt or were liquidated by 2001.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.860
And the ones who survived has
struggled to be profitable.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.200
Sometimes even requiring
government bailouts.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.960
- They've tried to make it up in fees.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.880
Perhaps you've noticed there are fees for

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.180
everything these days from checking bags

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:41.610
to changing your flight.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.940
This has become a multi-billion
dollars side hustle

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.660
for airlines, but unfortunately for them,

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:49.060
it hasn't been enough.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:52.210
The airlines still see
thin profit margins,

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.440
in the years they come out ahead at all.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:58.130
- And that's why the amazing
service of the golden age

00:05:58.130 --> 00:05:59.960
has declined.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.210
Airlines simply don't have the funds

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:06.170
or incentives to lavish
passengers in luxury.

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:08.230
But now the costs are so low,

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:09.570
a lot more people can travel.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.480
Which has led to crowded airports,

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:14.510
long lines and sitting closer to strangers

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:16.430
than any of us would prefer.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:19.190
This is the complete
opposite of the golden age.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:21.710
When planes used to be much less packed

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:24.020
since only the wealthy could afford it.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:27.120
Now you're lucky if this seat
next to you is even empty.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.600
- Seat pitches have also
shrunk over the years.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.860
This is the distance between your seat

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.490
and the seat in front of you.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:37.880
It has decreased by four to
seven inches in an effort

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:40.220
to fit more people on the plane.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:41.550
Goodbye leg room.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:44.790
- If you really want to return
to the golden age service

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:47.750
of the past, you could
always fly first class.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:50.380
You'll just have to pay golden age prices.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.420
But there are ways around this.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.260
If you try to upgrade a
coach ticket to first class

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:58.070
the day of your flight, they
might offer you discounts,

00:06:58.070 --> 00:06:59.890
or you could buy a coach ticket

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:02.080
and use frequent flyer points to cover

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:03.650
a first class upgrade.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:06.580
- As we begin our final
descent on this episode,

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:08.840
we just wanna say, we get it.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:10.800
Modern air travel is a drag.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.870
No one wants to arrive at
an airport two hours early,

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:17.620
wait in line and sit in
uncomfortable seats, eating peanuts.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:19.600
That makes it easy to long for the past.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.620
But don't forget that the
past had problems too.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.370
From costly fairs to excessive layovers,

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:29.150
to the increased chance
of dying in a fiery crash.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:30.660
- That's quite a perk .

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:34.960
While we lost fancy cocktails
and food served on real China,

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.450
we gained safer, quicker,
more affordable air travel

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:40.630
with wifi.

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:42.290
Don't take it for granted.

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:43.950
- And that's our 2 cents.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:48.020
Have you ever wondered what
makes words, well, words?

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:51.140
Other Words, PBS' newest
show about language

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.410
and linguistics, has you covered.

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:55.760
- From determining the origins of language

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:59.270
to whether or not computers
can actually talk.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:02.300
Other Words, dives into
languages around the world

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:04.960
to uncover the fascinating, unexpected,

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.500
and sometimes weird stories
behind the human trait

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:10.280
we all take for granted.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:13.020
Check it out on Storied and
be sure to let them know

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:14.683
that Two Cents sent you.

00:08:15.567 --> 00:08:18.234
(upbeat music)

